window.LLM_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the shape of pred - target when pred is (4, 1) and target is (4,) ?","o":["(4,)","(4, 1)","(4, 4)","It raises a shape error"],"a":2,"w":"Shapes align from the right: (4, 1) and (1, 4) both stretch to (4, 4), with no error. The loss computed from it was 2.83 instead of 3.71."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In bf16, what is 1.0 + 0.001?","o":["1.0","1.001","1.0009765625","inf"],"a":0,"w":"With 7 mantissa bits, the gap just above 1.0 is 0.0078, so adding 0.001 rounds back to 1.0. fp16 gives 1.0009765625."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A timer around c = a @ b on CUDA tensors reads 0.05 ms, but with torch.cuda.synchronize() before stopping it reads 11.2 ms. Why?","o":["The first run was cached","The call only queued the work; the timer stopped before the GPU finished","synchronize() slows the GPU down","The CPU did the first multiply"],"a":1,"w":"CUDA operations are asynchronous. Without a synchronise (or CUDA events), you measure only the launch."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On this GPU, how much faster was a 4096 × 4096 matmul in bf16 than in plain fp32?","o":["About the same","About 1.4 times","About 2.8 times","About 130 times"],"a":2,"w":"88.8 against 31.6 TFLOPS. About 130 times is bf16 on the GPU against fp32 on the CPU; TF32 gave about 1.4 times."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why do training loops pin the memory of their input batches?","o":["Pinned memory uses fewer bytes","It stops batches being shuffled","The GPU cannot read pageable memory at all","Copies from pinned memory are faster and can overlap with compute"],"a":3,"w":"1 GiB copied in 18.6 ms from pinned memory (57.6 GB/s) against 48.7 ms from pageable memory (22 GB/s), and a non_blocking copy from pinned memory runs alongside compute."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why must a training loop call zero_grad() every step?","o":["It resets the learning rate","backward() adds to .grad , so old gradients would pile up","It frees the model's weights","It is only needed on the first step"],"a":1,"w":"Gradients accumulate by design. Without clearing them, each update uses the sum of all gradients so far; measured, the loss ended at 13,551 instead of 65."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The stability limit for plain gradient descent on the latency problem was 0.1015. What happened at a learning rate of 0.12?","o":["It converged faster than 0.1","It converged to a different answer","It stalled at the starting loss","The loss grew every step until it overflowed to infinity"],"a":3,"w":"Above the limit each step overshoots by more than the last: 6.8 million at step 10, then infinity."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An overfit-one-batch test gets stuck with the loss at exactly the variance of the targets. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The learning rate is too small","The model has too many parameters","The loss compares predictions with the wrong targets, for example through a broadcasting bug","The GPU ran out of memory"],"a":2,"w":"When every prediction is compared with every target, the best possible answer is the mean, and the loss is then the targets' variance: 0.6424 here."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did the forward pass with gradients use 704 MiB more memory but only 128 MiB without them?","o":["Autograd keeps intermediate activations for the backward pass, plus the weight gradients","Gradients are always stored in fp64","torch.no_grad() compresses the weights","The model is copied for the backward pass"],"a":0,"w":"Seven saved activations of 64 MiB, a 128 MiB fp32 copy of the output for the loss, and 128 MiB of weight gradients. Without gradients, each activation can be freed as soon as the next layer has used it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does the cost of reverse-mode autograd compare with finite differences for a model with N parameters?","o":["Both need about N forward passes","Autograd needs about two forward passes' worth for all N; finite differences need about 2N forward passes","Finite differences are cheaper but less accurate","Autograd needs one backward pass per parameter"],"a":1,"w":"One backward pass yields every gradient at once, which is what makes training models with billions of parameters possible."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The naive softmax exp(z) / exp(z).sum() returned NaN on GPT-2's next-token logits even in fp32. Why?","o":["The largest logit overflowed to infinity","Every logit was around −124 or lower, so every exp() underflowed to 0 and the result was 0 / 0","The vocabulary is too large for fp32","GPT-2's logits contain NaN"],"a":1,"w":"exp(−124) ≈ 10⁻⁵⁴ is far below fp32's smallest value. Subtracting the max (−124.0) puts the largest term at exp(0) = 1."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Above roughly what value does exp(x) overflow to infinity in fp16?","o":["2","88.7","11.1","65,504"],"a":2,"w":"fp16's largest value is 65,504, and ln(65,504) ≈ 11.09. In fp32 and bf16 the limit is about 88.7."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"GPT-2 gave the correct next token a probability of 0.1592. What is that token's cross-entropy loss?","o":["About 1.84","About 0.16","About 0.84","About 6.28"],"a":0,"w":"−ln(0.1592) ≈ 1.84 nats, the value measured for \" 200\"."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A new language model with a 50,257-token vocabulary should have a loss close to what at step zero?","o":["0","1","3.9","10.8, which is ln(50,257)"],"a":3,"w":"Near-uniform predictions score ln(V) on every token; a freshly initialised head measured 11.02."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does RMSNorm differ from LayerNorm?","o":["It normalises across the batch instead of across channels","It divides by the root mean square without subtracting the mean, and has no bias","It has no learned parameters at all","It is applied only to the output layer"],"a":1,"w":"Both act on each token's vector. RMSNorm keeps only the rescaling and a learned gain; on GPT-2's vector the mean was already near zero (0.03)."}];
